<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#shudra">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shudra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shudra is the fourth of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>, the classes of traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> society described in Vedic and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> texts, below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishya">Vaishyas</a>. Shudras were assigned the duty of serving the other three classes and were excluded from Vedic study and ritual. Several ancient dynasties, including the Nandas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, were said in hostile sources to be of Shudra origin.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Shudra is one of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> class system in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The word appears once in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, in the Purusha Sukta, a creation story in which the four varnas form from the body of a primeval man: the brahmin from his mouth, the kshatriya from his arms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishya">vaishya</a> from his thighs and the shudra from his feet. This verse is now generally considered a later insertion, possibly a charter myth. The historian Ram Sharan Sharma suggests that it may have been meant to show a common lineage for all the varnas.</p><p>Stephanie W. Jamison and Joel Brereton find no evidence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> for an elaborate, much-subdivided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system and describe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> system there as embryonic, a social ideal rather than a social reality. According to Sharma, the Shudra class arose from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-aryans">Indo-Aryans</a> and non-Indo-Aryans relegated to that position through external and internal conflicts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The legal texts list disabilities for shudras. They were barred from studying and hearing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> and from performing Vedic sacrifices, although they could hear the Itihasa and Purana texts and build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, temples and parks. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manusmriti">Manusmriti</a>, according to Patrick Olivelle, allows brahmins to seize property from shudras, who own nothing, and says shudras should not accumulate wealth. The texts vary widely: the Apastamba Grhyasutra excludes shudra students from hearing the Vedas, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> states that all four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> may hear them.</p><p>Traditionally shudras were peasants and artisans, and their duty was service of the other three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>. Scholars note that this is a theoretical account found in select texts. G. C. Ghurye argues that in reality the hereditary occupations of the varnas were missing from large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and that members of all four were farmers, traders or warriors as opportunity required. Some early texts describe shudras as ministers and kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Evidence from medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> is mixed. Cynthia Talbot notes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> is rarely mentioned in the medieval records of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-eaton">Richard Eaton</a> finds that in the Kakatiya population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> between the 11th and 14th centuries shudras were part of the nobility and that fathers and sons often had different professions. Several poet-saints of the Bhakti movement, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukaram">Tukaram</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namdev">Namdev</a>, were born in shudra families, and Sikh Gurus included sixty of Namdev's compositions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>.</p><p>Bhim Rao Ambedkar argued that the shudras were originally kshatriyas denied the initiation ritual of upanayana by the brahmins, a claim Sharma contested. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> communities, the Soedra have typically been temple priests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shudra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Indian and Greek sources agree that the first Nanda was of humble origin: the Puranas call him the son of a <b>Shudra</b> woman, and the Roman historian Quintus Curtius Rufus reports that the founder of the dynasty was a barber.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The law books describe four varnas, or classes: Brahmins, priests and teachers; Kshatriyas, rulers and warriors; Vaishyas, merchants and farmers; and <b>Shudras</b>, labourers and servants.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Early on the tribal chief (rajan) and nobility (rajanya) stood at the top, the common people (viś) in the middle and the dasa and dasyu at the bottom; the Rigveda does not yet link the words Brahmana and Kshatriya to varna, and Vaishya and <b>Shudra</b> are absent.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Some hold that the Uttara Kanda contradicts the rest of the epic's picture of Rama and dharma; M. R. Parameswaran argues that its treatment of women and <b>Shudras</b>, in the stories of Sita's abandonment and the killing of the ascetic Shambuka, shows it to be a later insertion.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>The varna scheme of the ancient texts placed the Brahmins, the priests, at the head of four orders, above the Kshatriyas (rulers and warriors), the Vaishyas (traders) and the <b>Shudras</b> (labourers), with Dalits and Adivasis outside.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>The Brahmanical law books of this age, the Dharmashastras such as the Manusmriti, ranked people in four varnas fixed by birth: Brahmins, Kshatriyas, Vaishyas and <b>Shudras</b>, with groups such as the Chandalas placed outside them.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li></ul></section>
